---
export interface Props {
  slug: string;
}

const { slug } = Astro.props;
---

{slug === 'what-are-amps' && (
<section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="edu-main">
  <article class="content-card">
    <div class="section-divider">
      <div class="icon-circle" aria-hidden="true">A</div>
      <h2 id="edu-main">What Is an Amp (Ampere)?</h2>
      <div class="line" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    </div>
    <p class="content-lead"><strong>An amp (ampere) is the SI base unit of electric current.</strong> It measures the rate at which electric charge flows through a conductor. One ampere equals one coulomb of charge passing a point in one second. The symbol is <strong>A</strong>.</p>

    <h3>The SI Definition of the Ampere</h3>
    <p>Since 2019, the ampere has been defined by fixing the value of the elementary charge <em>e</em> to exactly 1.602 176 634 × 10⁻¹⁹ coulombs. This means one ampere is the flow of 1 ÷ 1.602 176 634 × 10⁻¹⁹ elementary charges per second — approximately 6.241 × 10¹⁸ electrons passing a point each second.</p>
    <p>Before 2019, the ampere was defined by the force between two parallel conductors carrying current. The modern definition, adopted at the 26th General Conference on Weights and Measures, ties the ampere to a fundamental physical constant rather than a mechanical measurement.</p>

    <h3>The Water Pipe Analogy</h3>
    <p>The most intuitive way to understand amps is the <strong>water pipe analogy</strong>:</p>
    <div class="home-example-grid">
      <div>
        <h4>Amps = Water flow rate</h4>
        <p>Amps measure how much electricity flows — like liters per second through a pipe. More amps means more current flowing.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4>Volts = Water pressure</h4>
        <p>Voltage is the pressure pushing the current. Higher voltage pushes more current through the same resistance, like higher water pressure pushes more water.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4>Resistance = Pipe diameter</h4>
        <p>Resistance (ohms) restricts current flow. A narrow pipe (high resistance) allows less water through at the same pressure.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4>Watts = Work done</h4>
        <p>Power is the result — how much work the flowing current does. More flow (amps) at higher pressure (volts) does more work (watts).</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>How Amps Relate to Volts, Watts, and Ohms</h3>
    <p>Amps do not exist in isolation. They connect to every other electrical quantity through two fundamental laws:</p>
    <div class="home-formula-grid">
      <div class="home-formula-card">
        <span><a href="/ohms-law-explained/">Ohm's Law</a></span>
        <strong>I = V ÷ R</strong>
        <p>Current (amps) equals voltage divided by resistance. Double the voltage with the same resistance, and current doubles.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="home-formula-card">
        <span><a href="/what-are-watts/">Watt's Law</a></span>
        <strong>P = I × V</strong>
        <p>Power (watts) equals current times voltage. This is the basis of every <a href="/">amps to watts conversion</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Common Amperage Values in Everyday Life</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="reference-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Context</th>
            <th scope="col">Typical Current</th>
            <th scope="col">Notes</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>LED light bulb</td><td>0.08 A</td><td>10W bulb on 120V</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Smartphone charger</td><td>1–3 A</td><td>USB-C fast charging at 5V</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Household outlet (US)</td><td>15–20 A</td><td>Circuit breaker limit, not typical draw</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Electric vehicle charger</td><td>32–48 A</td><td>Level 2 at 240V</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Car starter motor</td><td>150–400 A</td><td>Brief surge, 12V battery</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Lightning bolt</td><td>~20,000 A</td><td>Lasts microseconds</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Microchip transistor</td><td>Nanoamps</td><td>10⁻⁹ A — billionths of an amp</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>AC Amps vs DC Amps</h3>
    <p>In <strong>DC</strong> circuits (batteries, solar panels, electronics), current flows in one direction at a steady value. The amp reading is straightforward.</p>
    <p>In <strong>AC</strong> circuits (wall outlets, motors, grid power), current alternates direction 50 or 60 times per second. The amp value shown on meters is the <strong>RMS (root mean square)</strong> value — the equivalent DC current that would produce the same heating effect. The peak AC current is √2 (≈ 1.414) times higher than the RMS value.</p>

    <h3>How to Measure Amps</h3>
    <p>Amps are measured with an <strong>ammeter</strong> or <strong>clamp meter</strong>. An ammeter must be connected in series with the circuit (current flows through it). A clamp meter measures the magnetic field around a conductor without breaking the circuit — this is the preferred method for most practical measurements.</p>
    <p>Important: never connect an ammeter in parallel across a voltage source. Ammeters have near-zero resistance, and connecting one across a power source creates a short circuit.</p>

    <h3>Why Amps Matter for Safety</h3>
    <p>Current flow (amps) is what makes electricity dangerous to humans, not voltage alone. As little as <strong>0.1 amps (100 milliamps)</strong> through the heart can be fatal. This is why electrical safety focuses on preventing current flow through the body using insulation, grounding, GFCIs, and proper circuit protection.</p>
    <p>Circuit breakers and fuses are rated in amps because they protect wiring from overheating. A 20A breaker trips when more than 20 amps flows through the circuit, preventing the wire insulation from melting and causing a fire.</p>

    <h3>Converting Amps to Other Units</h3>
    <div class="home-formula-grid">
      <div class="home-formula-card">
        <span><a href="/">Amps to Watts</a></span>
        <strong>Watts = Amps × Volts</strong>
        <p>Multiply by voltage to find power. <a href="/120v-amps-to-watts-calculator/">10A at 120V = 1,200W</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="home-formula-card">
        <span>Amps to Milliamps</span>
        <strong>mA = Amps × 1,000</strong>
        <p>Multiply by 1,000 to convert to milliamps. 0.5A = 500 mA.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="home-formula-card">
        <span><a href="/amp-hours-to-watt-hours/">Amps to Amp-Hours</a></span>
        <strong>Ah = Amps × Hours</strong>
        <p>Multiply by time to find charge. A 5A load running for 10 hours = 50 Ah.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</section>
)}

{slug === 'what-are-watts' && (
<section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="edu-main">
  <article class="content-card">
    <div class="section-divider">
      <div class="icon-circle" aria-hidden="true">W</div>
      <h2 id="edu-main">What Is a Watt?</h2>
      <div class="line" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    </div>
    <p class="content-lead"><strong>A watt is the SI unit of power.</strong> It measures the rate at which energy is transferred, converted, or consumed. One watt equals one joule of energy per second. Named after Scottish inventor James Watt, the symbol is <strong>W</strong>.</p>

    <h3>The Formal Definition</h3>
    <p>One watt is defined as one joule per second (1 W = 1 J/s). In electrical terms, one watt is the power produced when one ampere of current flows through a potential difference of one volt:</p>
    <div class="home-formula-card" style="max-width:480px">
      <span>Watt definition</span>
      <strong>1 W = 1 V × 1 A = 1 J/s</strong>
      <p>Power = Voltage × Current = Energy per unit time</p>
    </div>
    <p>Watts measure instantaneous power — how fast energy is being used <em>right now</em>. This is different from energy (measured in watt-hours or kilowatt-hours), which measures total energy consumed over time.</p>

    <h3>Watts vs Watt-Hours: Power vs Energy</h3>
    <p>This distinction is crucial and frequently confused:</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="reference-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Concept</th>
            <th scope="col">Watts (W)</th>
            <th scope="col">Watt-Hours (Wh)</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>What it measures</td><td>Rate of energy use (power)</td><td>Total energy consumed</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Analogy</td><td>Speed of a car (km/h)</td><td>Distance traveled (km)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Example</td><td>A 100W bulb uses energy at 100 W</td><td>Running 10 hours = 1,000 Wh = 1 kWh</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Your electricity bill</td><td>Not directly billed</td><td>Billed per kWh consumed</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>The Watt Formula (Watt's Law)</h3>
    <p>Watts connect amps and volts through <strong>Watt's Law</strong>, the power equation:</p>
    <div class="home-formula-grid">
      <div class="home-formula-card">
        <span>Standard form</span>
        <strong>P = V × I</strong>
        <p>Watts = Volts × Amps. The most common formula for electrical power calculation.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="home-formula-card">
        <span>From resistance (Ohm's + Watt's)</span>
        <strong>P = I² × R</strong>
        <p>Watts = Amps² × Ohms. Useful when you know current and resistance but not voltage.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="home-formula-card">
        <span>From resistance (alternate)</span>
        <strong>P = V² ÷ R</strong>
        <p>Watts = Volts² ÷ Ohms. Useful when you know voltage and resistance but not current.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Common Wattage Values</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="reference-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Item</th>
            <th scope="col">Typical Watts</th>
            <th scope="col">Context</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>LED bulb (60W equivalent)</td><td>8–10 W</td><td>Same light, 85% less power</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Laptop</td><td>30–65 W</td><td>Varies with workload</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Desktop computer</td><td>200–500 W</td><td>Gaming PCs use more</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Microwave oven</td><td>800–1,200 W</td><td>Cooking power, not standby</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Hair dryer</td><td>1,000–1,800 W</td><td>High heat setting</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Space heater</td><td>1,500 W</td><td>Standard portable unit</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Electric oven</td><td>2,000–5,000 W</td><td>240V circuit in US</td></tr>
          <tr><td>EV charger (Level 2)</td><td>3,800–11,500 W</td><td>240V, 16A–48A</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Solar panel</td><td>300–600 W</td><td>Peak output under ideal sun</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Wind turbine (residential)</td><td>1,000–10,000 W</td><td>Depends on wind and size</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Kilowatts, Megawatts, and Gigawatts</h3>
    <p>For larger power values, metric prefixes are used:</p>
    <div class="home-example-grid">
      <div>
        <h4>Kilowatt (kW) = 1,000 W</h4>
        <p>Used for household appliances, small generators, solar arrays, and EV chargers. A typical home uses 1–2 kW average power.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4>Megawatt (MW) = 1,000,000 W</h4>
        <p>Used for industrial facilities, wind turbines (2–15 MW each), data centers, and utility power plants. A <a href="/megawatts-to-amps-calculator/">megawatts to amps calculator</a> converts MW to current.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4>Gigawatt (GW) = 1,000,000,000 W</h4>
        <p>Used for national-scale power generation. A large nuclear plant produces about 1 GW. Total US generation capacity is approximately 1,200 GW.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Real Watts vs Apparent Power (VA)</h3>
    <p>In AC circuits, watts and volt-amps (VA) can differ. <strong>Watts are real power</strong> — the portion that does useful work. <strong>VA is apparent power</strong> — the total current × voltage product. The ratio is <a href="/understanding-power-factor/">power factor</a>: Watts = VA × PF.</p>
    <p>This matters for generators, UPS systems, and transformers, which are often rated in VA or kVA rather than watts. A 1,000 VA UPS at power factor 0.70 delivers only 700 watts of real power. Use the <a href="/kva-to-watts-calculator/">kVA to watts calculator</a> for accurate conversions.</p>

    <h3>How to Calculate Watts from Amps</h3>
    <p>The most common use of watts is converting from amps: <strong>Watts = Amps × Volts</strong>. Use the <a href="/">Amps to Watts Calculator</a> for instant conversions including DC, single-phase AC, and three-phase AC with power factor support.</p>
  </article>
</section>
)}

{slug === 'ohms-law-explained' && (
<section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="edu-main">
  <article class="content-card">
    <div class="section-divider">
      <div class="icon-circle" aria-hidden="true">Ω</div>
      <h2 id="edu-main">Ohm's Law Explained</h2>
      <div class="line" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    </div>
    <p class="content-lead"><strong>Ohm's Law states that voltage equals current multiplied by resistance: V = I × R.</strong> Discovered by German physicist Georg Simon Ohm in 1827, this fundamental relationship connects the three primary electrical quantities and forms the basis for all circuit analysis.</p>

    <h3>The Three Forms of Ohm's Law</h3>
    <p>The same relationship can be rearranged to solve for any unknown when the other two values are known:</p>
    <div class="home-formula-grid">
      <div class="home-formula-card">
        <span>Find Voltage</span>
        <strong>V = I × R</strong>
        <p>Voltage (volts) = Current (amps) × Resistance (ohms). Example: 5A through 24Ω = 120V.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="home-formula-card">
        <span>Find Current</span>
        <strong>I = V ÷ R</strong>
        <p>Current (<a href="/what-are-amps/">amps</a>) = Voltage ÷ Resistance. Example: 120V across 24Ω = 5A.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="home-formula-card">
        <span>Find Resistance</span>
        <strong>R = V ÷ I</strong>
        <p>Resistance (ohms) = Voltage ÷ Current. Example: 120V with 5A flowing = 24Ω.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>The Power Wheel (Ohm's Law + Watt's Law)</h3>
    <p>Combining Ohm's Law with <a href="/what-are-watts/">Watt's Law</a> (P = V × I) gives 12 useful formulas organized in the "power wheel." Here are the most practical combinations:</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="reference-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Find</th>
            <th scope="col">Know V and I</th>
            <th scope="col">Know V and R</th>
            <th scope="col">Know I and R</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Voltage (V)</strong></td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>V = I × R</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Current (I)</strong></td><td>—</td><td>I = V ÷ R</td><td>—</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Resistance (R)</strong></td><td>R = V ÷ I</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Power (W)</strong></td><td>P = V × I</td><td>P = V² ÷ R</td><td>P = I² × R</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Worked Examples</h3>
    <div class="home-example-grid">
      <div>
        <h4>Example 1: Finding current draw</h4>
        <p>A 120V circuit has a 1,440W heater. What current does it draw? Using P = V × I, rearranged: I = P ÷ V = 1,440 ÷ 120 = <strong>12 amps</strong>. This fits on a 15A circuit within the 80% continuous load rule.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4>Example 2: Finding resistance</h4>
        <p>A 12V LED strip draws 3A. What is its resistance? R = V ÷ I = 12 ÷ 3 = <strong>4 ohms</strong>. This determines the heat generated: P = I² × R = 9 × 4 = 36 watts.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4>Example 3: Voltage drop on wire</h4>
        <p>A wire has 0.5Ω resistance and carries 10A. What voltage is lost? V = I × R = 10 × 0.5 = <strong>5 volts</strong> drop. On a 12V system, that is a 42% loss — far too high. Use the <a href="/voltage-drop-calculator/">voltage drop calculator</a> to size wire properly.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4>Example 4: Power from resistance</h4>
        <p>A 240V oven element has 11.5Ω resistance. Power: P = V² ÷ R = 57,600 ÷ 11.5 = <strong>5,009 watts (5 kW)</strong>. Current: I = V ÷ R = 240 ÷ 11.5 = 20.9A → needs a 30A circuit.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>When Ohm's Law Does Not Apply</h3>
    <p>Ohm's Law applies to <strong>ohmic (linear) materials</strong> where resistance is constant. It does not directly apply to:</p>
    <div class="home-example-grid">
      <div>
        <h4>Diodes and LEDs</h4>
        <p>Non-linear resistance — current increases exponentially with voltage above the forward voltage threshold.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4>Transistors</h4>
        <p>Current depends on a control signal (base/gate), not just voltage across the device.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4>Superconductors</h4>
        <p>Resistance drops to zero below critical temperature, so any voltage produces infinite current (limited by other factors).</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4>Arc welders / lightning</h4>
        <p>Plasma arcs have negative resistance characteristics — resistance decreases as current increases.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>For AC circuits with inductors and capacitors, Ohm's Law extends to impedance (Z) instead of simple resistance: V = I × Z, where Z includes both resistive and reactive components.</p>

    <h3>Ohm's Law and the Amps to Watts Formula</h3>
    <p>The <a href="/">amps to watts formula</a> (W = A × V) is a direct application of Watt's Law, which works alongside Ohm's Law. Together, they allow you to calculate any electrical quantity from two known values. The <a href="/voltage-amps-watts-calculator/">Voltage Amps Watts Calculator</a> combines both laws into a single tool.</p>
  </article>
</section>
)}

{slug === 'understanding-power-factor' && (
<section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="edu-main">
  <article class="content-card">
    <div class="section-divider">
      <div class="icon-circle" aria-hidden="true">PF</div>
      <h2 id="edu-main">Understanding Power Factor</h2>
      <div class="line" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    </div>
    <p class="content-lead"><strong>Power factor (PF) is the ratio of real power (watts) to apparent power (volt-amps) in an AC circuit.</strong> It ranges from 0 to 1.00, where 1.00 means all the current drawn from the supply is doing useful work. A lower power factor means wasted current that generates heat without producing useful output.</p>

    <h3>The Power Triangle</h3>
    <p>AC power has three components that form a right triangle:</p>
    <div class="home-formula-grid">
      <div class="home-formula-card">
        <span>Real Power (P)</span>
        <strong>Measured in Watts (W)</strong>
        <p>The horizontal leg — actual useful power that does work (heat, light, motion). This is what you pay for on your electricity bill.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="home-formula-card">
        <span>Reactive Power (Q)</span>
        <strong>Measured in VAR (volt-amps reactive)</strong>
        <p>The vertical leg — power that oscillates between source and load, doing no useful work. Caused by inductors (motors) and capacitors.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="home-formula-card">
        <span>Apparent Power (S)</span>
        <strong>Measured in VA (volt-amps)</strong>
        <p>The hypotenuse — the total current × voltage product. This is what the utility must deliver and what generators, transformers, and UPS units are sized for.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>The relationship: <strong>S² = P² + Q²</strong> and <strong>PF = P ÷ S = cos(φ)</strong>, where φ is the phase angle between voltage and current waveforms.</p>

    <h3>Why Power Factor Matters</h3>
    <div class="home-example-grid">
      <div>
        <h4>Generator and UPS sizing</h4>
        <p>A 10 kVA generator at PF 0.80 delivers only 8 kW. If you need 10 kW, you must buy a 12.5 kVA generator. Ignoring PF means undersized equipment. Use the <a href="/kva-to-watts-calculator/">kVA to watts calculator</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4>Higher electricity costs</h4>
        <p>Industrial customers with PF below 0.85–0.90 face utility penalty surcharges. Low PF means the utility supplies extra current that heats conductors without generating revenue.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4>Larger wire and breakers</h4>
        <p>Low PF means higher current for the same real power. A 10 kW load at PF 1.00 draws 41.7A at 240V. At PF 0.70, it draws 59.5A — requiring larger wire and breakers.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4>Accurate amps to watts conversion</h4>
        <p>Without PF, you overestimate watts from amps. The <a href="/ac-amps-to-watts-calculator/">AC amps to watts calculator</a> includes a PF input for this reason: <strong>Watts = Amps × Volts × PF</strong>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Typical Power Factor Values by Equipment</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="reference-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Equipment</th>
            <th scope="col">Typical PF</th>
            <th scope="col">Type</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Resistive heater, incandescent lamp</td><td>1.00</td><td>Resistive (unity PF)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>LED with good driver</td><td>0.90 – 0.99</td><td>Active PFC corrected</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Fluorescent with electronic ballast</td><td>0.85 – 0.95</td><td>Corrected</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Computer PSU (with PFC)</td><td>0.90 – 0.99</td><td>Active PFC</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Computer PSU (no PFC)</td><td>0.55 – 0.75</td><td>Uncorrected</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Induction motor (full load)</td><td>0.80 – 0.90</td><td>Inductive lagging</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Induction motor (25% load)</td><td>0.40 – 0.60</td><td>Severely lagging at light load</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Induction motor (no load)</td><td>0.15 – 0.35</td><td>Almost all reactive</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Air conditioner / heat pump</td><td>0.80 – 0.95</td><td>Compressor motor</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Welding transformer</td><td>0.50 – 0.70</td><td>Highly inductive</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Variable frequency drive (VFD)</td><td>0.95 – 0.98</td><td>Rectifier input</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Synchronous motor (adjusted)</td><td>0.90 – 1.00</td><td>Can lead, lag, or unity</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Leading vs Lagging Power Factor</h3>
    <p><strong>Lagging PF</strong> means current lags behind voltage — caused by inductive loads (motors, transformers, solenoids). This is by far the most common type. <strong>Leading PF</strong> means current leads voltage — caused by capacitive loads (capacitor banks, some electronic power supplies). Power factor correction adds capacitors to counteract lagging inductive loads.</p>

    <h3>How to Correct Low Power Factor</h3>
    <div class="home-example-grid">
      <div>
        <h4>Capacitor banks</h4>
        <p>Install capacitors to supply reactive power locally, reducing the reactive current drawn from the utility. Calculate kVAR needed: kVAR = kW × (tan(φ₁) – tan(φ₂)), where φ₁ and φ₂ are the current and target phase angles.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4>Synchronous condensers</h4>
        <p>Over-excited synchronous motors or generators supply leading reactive power, improving PF. Used in large industrial plants and utility substations.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4>Active PFC in electronics</h4>
        <p>Modern power supplies use active power factor correction circuits to reshape input current to near-sinusoidal, achieving PF 0.95–0.99.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h4>Properly size motors</h4>
        <p>Oversized motors running at partial load have very low PF. Right-sizing motors to match the actual mechanical load improves PF and reduces energy waste.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Power Factor in the Amps to Watts Formula</h3>
    <p>Power factor directly appears in the AC amps to watts formulas:</p>
    <div class="home-formula-grid">
      <div class="home-formula-card">
        <span>Single-phase AC</span>
        <strong>W = A × V × PF</strong>
        <p>Without PF, a 10A motor at 240V looks like 2,400W. At PF 0.85, the real power is actually 2,040W.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="home-formula-card">
        <span>Three-phase AC</span>
        <strong>W = √3 × A × V × PF</strong>
        <p>For <a href="/3-phase-amps-to-watts-calculator/">three-phase systems</a>, PF has an even larger impact due to higher power levels.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>Use the <a href="/power-factor/">Power Factor Calculator</a> to find PF from real and apparent power measurements, or to convert between watts and VA.</p>
  </article>
</section>
)}
